SECTION A: MONTHS OF THE YEAR
Teach all 12 months:

January, February, March, April, May, June, July, August, September, October, November, December.

Activity 1 – Learn the Months
Display all 12 months with:

Correct English spelling.
Clear audio pronunciation.
Attractive cartoon illustrations.
Seasonal illustrations that help students remember the months.
Use the common Northern Hemisphere seasonal calendar as an approximate educational reference:

Winter: December, January, February.
Spring: March, April, May.
Summer: June, July, August.
Autumn: September, October, November.
Do not imply that these seasons describe the exact weather in Saudi Arabia.

Activity 2 – Month Quiz
Create varied, randomized questions:

Look at a seasonal illustration and choose the correct month.
Listen to a month and select its written name.
Match a month to its seasonal image.
Answer questions such as “What comes after March?”
Answer questions such as “What comes before October?”
Avoid repeating the same question format continuously.

Activity 3 – Put the Months in Order
This is a mandatory mastery activity.

Students must arrange all 12 months in the correct calendar order, from January to December.

Use an interactive drag-and-drop or tap-to-arrange activity.

Provide feedback and allow retries.

Critical rule: A student cannot be marked as having mastered Months of the Year unless she can correctly arrange all 12 months in their correct order without assistance.

Activity 4 – Say the Month
Allow students to use their microphone to say month names.

Record the student’s speech.
Evaluate pronunciation using an appropriate speech assessment capability.
Provide clear feedback.
Allow multiple attempts.
Track pronunciation progress separately.
Do not treat speech-to-text transcription alone as a reliable pronunciation score.

If the speech assessment capability is unavailable or uncertain, mark the result as requiring teacher review instead of falsely claiming accurate assessment.

Activity 5 – Spell the Month
Create an interactive spelling game.

Display a month image or audio prompt.
Show scrambled letters.
Allow the student to arrange letters into the correct spelling.
Give feedback.
Track spelling accuracy.
A future optional challenge may include spelling a month from audio without displaying its written form.

Months mastery criteria
Track these separately:

Month recognition.
Month sequence.
Pronunciation.
Spelling.
Full mastery requires successful completion of the 12-month ordering activity and the defined recognition, pronunciation, and spelling requirements.

SECTION B: NUMBERS – 1 TO 1,000
The educational objective is NOT memorizing every number individually.

The objective is to enable students to read and write numbers up to 1,000, especially three-digit numbers.

Teach the composition and reading of numbers:

Hundreds.
Tens.
Ones.
Numbers containing zero.
One thousand.
Use the American English number-writing style WITHOUT “and”.

Examples:

125 → One hundred twenty-five
308 → Three hundred eight
470 → Four hundred seventy
405 → Four hundred five
670 → Six hundred seventy
902 → Nine hundred two
1,000 → One thousand
Be consistent throughout all lessons, activities, feedback, and automated correction.

Activity 1 – Learn the Numbers
Teach number composition visually.

Show how hundreds, tens, and ones form a number.

Examples:

125 = 100 + 20 + 5

340 = 300 + 40

708 = 700 + 8

Use clear visual blocks, number cards, and simple explanations.

Activity 2 – Read the Number
Show a written number and ask students to:

Select the correct spoken form.
Listen to a number and choose its numerical form.
Read the number aloud where supported.
Use different numbers rather than repeatedly practicing the same examples.

Activity 3 – Write in Words
Show a number such as 256.

Ask the student to write:

Two hundred fifty-six.

Check spelling, word order, hyphenation where appropriate, and number composition.

Activity 4 – Number Dictation
Play a clear audio prompt.

Students must write the number using digits.

Example:

Audio: “Four hundred thirty-two.”

Answer: 432.

Include a second dictation challenge in which the student hears the number and writes it in English words.

Activity 5 – Number Challenge
Create randomized mixed questions:

Read a number.
Write a number in words.
Write a spoken number using digits.
Identify missing numbers in a sequence.
Distinguish tens and hundreds.
Identify numbers containing zero.
Recognize the difference between similar number forms.
Numbers mastery
Assess separately:

Number recognition.
Reading.
Writing in words.
Listening and number dictation.
Use varied numbers up to 1,000.

Do not mark a student as proficient simply because she memorized a small set of example numbers.

